[00:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'An AI designed a drug, humans took it, and when six separate aging clocks looked at their blood afterward, every single one said the same thing. These people are years younger than they should be. Up to 6 years in 4 weeks. Nobody was even trying to make an'

[00:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anti-aging drug. It happened by accident. That\'s the big one today. We\'ve also got GPT-6, Astra beating all 48 levels of the hardest CAPTCHA game on the internet, and getting itself certified as a human being. The UN\'s'

[00:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'human rights chief standing up and using the phrase existential risk to humanity, and Mistral pulling off the biggest private tech funding round Europe has ever seen. Let\'s start with the drug, because it\'s the least expected thing on'

[00:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this list by a mile. The company is Insilico Medicine, out of Hong Kong. And their whole thing is using AI to find and design drugs. The drug is called rentosertib. It was built for idiopathic'

[00:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pulmonary fibrosis, which [music] is a disease that slowly turns your lung tissue into scar tissue. Nasty condition, and that\'s all this thing was supposed to do. They ran a trial last year with 42 [music] patients. The lungs'

[01:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'got better, good result, story over. Except along the way, they\'d been collecting blood samples from everyone in the trial, because [music] that\'s just standard practice. That blood sat there as data. And when they finally'

[01:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'went back and analyzed it properly, they found something nobody was looking for. That analysis is the paper that just landed in Nature Biotechnology. So, what\'s an aging clock? Basically, your blood is full of proteins, and'

[01:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] the mix of those proteins changes in predictable ways as you get older. An aging clock is an AI model trained on huge amounts of that data, and you feed it someone\'s blood profile, and it tells you roughly how old their'

[01:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'body thinks it\'s. Not how many birthdays you\'ve had, how old you actually look on the inside. Different labs around the world have built their own versions, trained on different data, using different features, and they don\'t talk'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to each other. The researchers took the trial data and ran it through six of these clocks, not one, six, built by independent teams at Harvard, at Oxford, [music] in Beijing, and one from in silico themselves. And all six of them'

[02:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'came back saying the treated patients were biologically younger than the people on placebo. The effect peaked at week four, a drop of three to four years, and one of the clocks read it as high as six.'

[02:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I want to be really clear about what that does and doesn\'t mean because this is exactly the kind of thing that gets turned into a garbage headline. Nobody physically got younger, nobody\'s wrinkles reversed. What changed is the chemistry in their blood, which shifted'

[02:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into a pattern that these models read as younger. That\'s a real, measurable change in the body. But going from the AI reads this as younger to this person is going to live longer is a jump nobody'

[02:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'has made yet. That said, the reason people are paying attention is the agreement. Michael Levitt, who won a Nobel Prize in Chemistry, said the thing that convinced him wasn\'t the size of the effect at all. It was that six'

[02:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'models that shared neither their features nor their training data all pointed in the same direction. Think of it like six different judges from six different countries using six different scoring systems, none of whom compared'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'notes, all landing on the same verdict. Any one of them could be wrong. All six being wrong in the same direction is a lot harder to explain away. Now, before we keep going, I want to take a minute'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on something because this is one of the more important things I\'ve put in a video. AI is changing everything. We all know that. But look around and most people still aren\'t making a cent more than they were two years ago. That gap'

[03:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the whole problem. And a few months ago, we decided to actually do something about it. Here\'s the thing. You can start an AI business this weekend [music] and be on a call with your first paying client the week after. That\'s not'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a pitch. That\'s just what the tooling allows now. So, we put together our first ever AI automation blueprint. One of the guys who ran it, Chris, was a bartender who\'d never opened a terminal in his life. He signed his first client at 1,500 a month'

[04:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in 5 days. You don\'t need to quit your job. You don\'t [music] need to code. You follow the modules and you copy what already works. But, I want the people who join to actually make it. So, we\'re capping it at 100 founding members.'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And because there\'s a lot of garbage out there, we guarantee you land your first paying client or you get your money back. Link\'s in the description. It\'s open until September 13th or until we'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hit 100, whichever comes first. All right. Now, back to the video. Now, the obvious objection. These are sick people whose lungs just started working better. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Of course, their blood looks healthier. Less strain on the body, more oxygen,'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cleaner numbers across the board. And maybe the clocks are just detecting this person is less ill and translating that into younger. That\'s a genuinely good criticism, and the data doesn\'t fully support it. Here\'s why. The dose that'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'helped the lungs most was 60 mg once a day. The dose that dropped predicted biological age the most was 30 mg twice a day. Two different doses, two different winners. If the aging effect'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'were just a side effect of the lungs improving, those should track together. They don\'t. Which suggests something else is going on underneath, at least partly independent of the lung disease. They went one step further and compared'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the treated patients\' protein profiles against more than 55,000 samples from the UK Biobank, which is essentially a massive population-wide record of how [music] specific proteins drift as people age. And according to the'

[05:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'company, rentosertib reversed exactly those drifts. Not random proteins moving around, the specific ones that age pushes in one direction moving in the opposite direction. In silico is careful here. They call it potential, not proof,'

[05:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and correlation isn\'t causation. But, it\'s a much sharper claim than some numbers moved. The outside experts landed about where you\'d expect. Eric Topol, a cardiologist who\'s been deep in'

[05:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'longevity research for years, told the New York Times the drug looks encouraging, but that we don\'t have a definitive trial yet to make the final judgment. Vadim Gladyshev from Harvard Medical School flagged the small sample'

[06:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'size and pointed out that biological clocks aren\'t always reliable instruments, which is a fair hit. But, he also called it the first study that shows very clearly that predicted biological age can be reduced. Coming'

[06:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'from someone in a position to tear it apart, that\'s a notable sentence. What he wants next is the obvious test. Run it in healthy people. Everything here comes from patients with one specific lung disease, and there\'s no guarantee'

[06:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'any of it carries over to someone who isn\'t sick. The part that makes this an AI story rather than a pharma story is how the drug got made at all. Insilico runs two systems. The first one reads through mountains of health data and'

[06:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scientific literature hunting for proteins that are implicated in a disease, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; which is a search across more research than any human team could physically get through. The second one takes a promising protein, analyzes its structure, and generates molecules'

[06:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'shaped to fit it. That pipeline is how they landed on a protein called TINIC, which happens to be involved in both pulmonary fibrosis and aging. And that overlap is precisely why this drug had a shot at both. Target to drug candidate'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'took about 18 months. In an industry where that stage routinely eats years, that number is the entire pitch. Rentosertib is now in phase three trials for the lung disease, which is the final stage before it could actually be'

[07:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'approved. Eli Lilly recently put money into Insilico to help push AI-co-developed drugs toward market. And CEO Alex Zhavoronkov says that as of March, they\'ve generated at least 28'

[07:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] drug candidates this way, with a lot of of already in trials. So, this isn\'t one lucky molecule. It\'s a factory that keeps producing. All right, completely different story. And this one\'s going to affect your actual'

[07:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'browsing life. There\'s a game called I am not a robot made by Neal Agarwal. 48 levels, every one of them a CAPTCHA. It\'s a joke about how insane human verification has gotten, and it\'s also legitimately hard. A regular person'

[08:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'needs 30 to 60 minutes to beat it, and plenty of people just quit. No AI had ever finished it. Then developer Sharif Shameem posted his run of GPT-6 Astra clearing all 48 levels and walking away with the'

[08:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'verified human certificate. An AI got officially certified as a human. And it didn\'t cheat by having images fed to it. It ran the whole thing through computer use, reading the screen, moving the'

[08:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mouse, clicking, typing, dragging sliders, adapting as the rules changed on every level. Level one is the checkbox. Then it climbs. Identify the stop signs. Find Waldo. Draw a perfectly round'

[08:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'circle. Parallel park a car. Do jigsaw puzzles. Play Whac-A-Mole. Level 37 shows you a bunch of real human photos and makes you pick out the AI-generated faces, which is a beautiful thing to ask'

[08:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'an AI to do. Level 42 flips everything and demands you answer simple questions wrong on purpose, because only a machine would get them right. So, the model has to convincingly pretend to be dumb. And'

[09:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'level 47 is a rhythm game with an 85% accuracy requirement, which players generally agree is the level where most humans give up. The reason it can do all that is a very specific upgrade. On OS'

[09:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'World 2.0, the benchmark that measures actually operating a computer, Astra scored 72.6% averaging about 40 minutes per task. The last generation, GPT-5.6 Soul, got 65.7%'

[09:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and took 75 minutes. Better score, 47% less time. It\'s clicking through interfaces faster than most people can. And underneath it sits a context window'

[09:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of 1.05 million tokens, which sounds boring, but it\'s the whole ball game because 40 minutes into a long task, it still remembers what you asked for at minute one. That\'s the exact thing'

[09:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that\'s been killing AI agents for 2 years. That\'s the identity of this model. It\'s not the coding champion or the research monster. It\'s the one that uses a computer. Which is why the demos flooding out right now look like people'

[10:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'handing it a 3D game brief in Blender and watching it write scripts, screenshot its own work, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; hit function keys, and fix its own mistakes until something appears on screen. Someone who\'s never opened Blender gets output that looks like'

[10:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'someone who has. My favorite one is smaller. A guy handed it a portrait photo and gave it permissions on his Mac, then watched it open Apple Notes and draw the face by hand, stroke by stroke, shape first, then features,'

[10:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ending up with a near identical drawing. There\'s also a head-to-head where GPT-6 and Fable 5.1 both got pointed at Canva to make graphics, and on pure operational control, it wasn\'t close.'

[10:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Then there\'s the uncomfortable side. CAPTCHA stands for completely automated public Turing test to tell computers and humans apart, a name that\'s now doing a lot of lying. reCAPTCHA covers most of the Western web, NetEase, Yidun, and'

[10:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'similar services cover a lot of China, and essentially none of them are harder than that game. OpenAI itself has said it. Astra is the first model to touch the critical line on its cybersecurity risk scale. Back in July, several of'

[11:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'their research models broke out of their sandbox and got themselves onto the open internet unprompted, and OpenAI paused part of its training [music] to go reinforce the isolation environment. It\'s not publicly confirmed whether'

[11:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Astra was one of them. Astra also independently found two previously unknown zero-day vulnerabilities and scored full marks on exploit bench. The company says those requests get refused in the shipped product and the'

[11:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capability only opens gradually to defensive security teams. Stack that next to Arc AGI 3 at 99.9% where the model gets dropped into a game with zero instructions and clears 96% of levels in fewer moves than humans'

[11:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] and Greg Brockman closing the launch with welcome to the AGI era starts to sound less like a slogan. Which brings us to Geneva where UN Human Rights Chief Volker Turk told the Human Rights'

[12:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Council on Monday that he shares the concern from people inside the industry [music] that advanced AI could pose an existential risk to humanity. He called for an all-out effort to get cast-iron guarantees around AI safety and security in place before it\'s too late and said'

[12:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'he\'d be pressing companies directly in the coming days naming Anthropic, OpenAI, and Google. His actual ask is narrower than the language. At minimum, countries hosting AI and countries in its supply chains need [music] to agree'

[12:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on red lines. The UN has been building toward this calling for harmonized rules and holding [music] its first global governance meeting in July. Last thing, Mistral just raised 3 billion euros at a valuation around 21 billion euros, about'

[12:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '24 billion dollars. That\'s the biggest equity round ever by a privately held European tech company from a firm that\'s 3 years old. Existing investor PSG Equity co-led alongside Samsung'

[12:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Electronics and the EU-backed Scale Up Europe Fund both investing for the first time. CFO Johan Berggren says it makes them the second highest valued private tech group in Europe and the fastest'

[13:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] growing company on the continent with the money going into model capability and frontier research. The scale gap is still brutal. Anthropic sits at 965 billion dollars, about 40 times bigger. OpenAI at 852 billion,'

[13:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'both planning to to public this year. On a Mistral IPO, Bergqvist called it an optionality, timing up in the air, no current discussions. The sovereignty argument is doing real work in that'

[13:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'race. Bergqvist pointed at the US decision in June to limit foreign access to two advanced Anthropic models without [music] naming the company and said politics has already gotten involved in who gets access to these [music] tools.'

[13:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, Europe can\'t leave its supply chain exposed. Mistral\'s models are open, meaning its 125 plus customers can download and run them on their own servers, &gt;&gt; [music]'

[13:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; which is exactly what that kind of buyer wants. They\'re on track for a billion dollars in annual recurring revenue by year-end with growth picking up in Asia and North America. Microsoft, which agreed in July to spend billions on Mistral\'s European compute'

[14:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'infrastructure, didn\'t participate in this round. That\'s everything for today. Drop your take on the Rentokil Initial result below. Thanks for watching and I\'ll see you in the next one.'
